    <string name="settings_help_icons">Show help icons</string>
    <string name="help_tracking">Choose what you record for each set. Weight and reps needs both values. Bodyweight records reps and any added weight; enter 0 when there is none. Other modes record reps, duration, distance, or duration and distance. Past workouts keep their original tracking.</string>
    <string name="help_aliases">Alternate names for the same exercise, separated by commas. Search and imports use them to find the right exercise. For example, Bench and Benkpress can both point to Bench press.</string>
    <string name="help_increment">The load step used by the keypad and to round suggested weights for this exercise. For example, an increment of 2.5 rounds a suggested 61 to 60, or 62 to 62.5.</string>
    <string name="help_unit_override">Default follows Training settings. Choose kg or lb here to keep new workouts for this exercise in that unit. Earlier workouts retain their recorded units.</string>
    <string name="help_supersets">Give exercises the same group name, such as A, to link them in a rotation. With automatic superset advance enabled, completing a set focuses the next unfinished exercise in that group.</string>
    <string name="help_effort">RPE rates how hard a set felt on a 1–10 scale. RIR means reps in reserve: how many more reps you think you could have done with good form.\n\nFor example, RPE 8 is roughly 2 RIR. RPE 10 or 0 RIR means no more reps were available. These fields are optional.</string>
    <string name="help_set_types">Working is a main training set. Warmup marks a preparation set and is excluded from statistics by default. Drop labels a lighter follow-on set. Failure marks a set taken to your limit; AMRAP means as many reps as possible. Enter the reps you actually completed for every type.</string>
    <string name="help_load_targets">Absolute is a specific weight. % 1RM and % TM calculate a weight from your one-rep max or training max. RPE and RIR prescribe effort instead of calculating a weight.\n\nExample: 80% of a 100 kg 1RM gives 80 kg before increment rounding.</string>
    <string name="help_progression">None turns off progression suggestions. Linear adds load after all targets are met. Double builds reps within a range, then adds load and resets reps to the bottom. Manual uses your weekly table.\n\nExample: for 3 sets of 8–12, reach 12 on all three sets before adding load and returning to 8. Review and accept suggestions to apply them.</string>
    <string name="help_frequency">Per session can suggest changes for the exercise’s next appearance in the plan. Per week waits until its last appearance that week. Suggestions still need your acceptance.</string>
    <string name="help_deload">For linear or double progression, a deload reduces the next suggested load by a percentage. A 10% deload changes 50 to 45 before rounding. It can follow consecutive missed targets or recur every N weeks. Review the suggestion before applying it.</string>
    <string name="help_plan_days">Days are ordered workouts in a repeating rotation, rather than fixed calendar dates. Advance through them after finishing workouts, or enable automatic plan advance. Choose week and day lets you resume at another point.</string>
    <string name="help_week_overrides">Replace an exercise’s target sets for a particular week, optionally for one plan day. Use this for a planned lighter week or a different rep scheme. All days applies the override wherever that exercise appears in the selected week.</string>
    <string name="help_rule_overrides">Set a different progression rule for one exercise. Other exercises continue to use the plan’s default rule. For example, one exercise can use linear progression while the others use double progression.</string>
    <string name="help_manual_weeks">Enter one target per line as week, weight, reps, using the rule’s selected unit. For example, 2,50,8 means week 2 uses a weight of 50 and 8 reps. Leave weight or reps blank to keep that part of the existing target.</string>
    <string name="help_custom_fields">Add extra values to record for each set. Use one line per field: key | label | number/text/boolean | unit.\n\nFor example: tempo | Tempo | text |\n\nKeys must be unique. A boolean becomes an on/off value. These fields appear in the set’s options.</string>
    <string name="help_prefill">Copies values from your previous completed sets into a new routine workout. The previous-results setting chooses which workout to use. Workouts started from a plan use its prescribed targets instead. Tick a set only after completing it.</string>
    <string name="help_previous_global">When enabled, previous values come from the most recent finished workout containing that exercise, even if it used another routine. When disabled, previous values come from the same routine.</string>
    <string name="help_suggestions">Review these targets before your next workout. You can accept one set, an exercise, or everything. Accepted targets apply at the exercise’s next occurrence in the plan.</string>
    <string name="help_auto_advance">Moves the plan’s position to its next day when you finish a workout started from that plan. It does not accept progression suggestions. When disabled, use Advance plan from the workout summary.</string>
    <string name="help_warmups">Enables Add warmups in an exercise’s menu during a workout. It generates lighter sets from the first working-set weight and rounds them to the exercise’s increment. Generated sets are marked Warmup.</string>
    <string name="help_e1rm">e1RM means estimated one-rep maximum, calculated from the weight and reps of a completed set. Epley is the default formula; the Brzycki setting changes this estimate for charts and personal records. An estimate can differ from a tested single rep.</string>
    <string name="help_volume">Strength volume is weight × reps, added across completed weight-and-rep sets. Warmups are excluded unless enabled in Training settings. Bodyweight and cardio sets are recorded separately from this volume total.</string>
    <string name="help_plates">Add each plate weight and enter the total number you own across both sides. For example, four 20 kg plates means two pairs. Set the quantity to zero for plates you no longer use. Enter the bar weight separately; calculator results show plates for each side.</string>
    <string name="help_maxes">1RM is your one-rep maximum. Training max (TM) is the reference weight for % TM targets.\n\nExample: 80% of a 90 kg training max gives 72 kg before rounding to your exercise’s increment. Leave a value blank to use an estimate from history when available.</string>
    <string name="help_exact_alarms">Allows rest alerts to stay on time with the screen off or when Android closes the app. Enable this access in Android’s Alarms &amp; reminders settings. Workout logging remains available without it.</string>
    <string name="help_logging_title">Logging sets</string>
    <string name="help_logging">Previous shows results from an earlier finished workout. Tap a value to enter what you did, then tick the set to mark it complete. Prefilled values are starting points.\n\nTap a set number for its type, units, RPE/RIR and extra fields. Long press the number to reorder sets, or swipe a row left to delete it. Undo restores deleted sets.</string>
    <string name="help_density">Converting between volume and weight depends on the ingredient: a cup of flour weighs less than a cup of water. The ≈ mark means an estimated ingredient density was used. Edit densities to match your ingredients. The editable value is grams per millilitre: 1 means 100 ml weighs 100 g. Conversion changes the display; your saved recipe stays intact.</string>
    <string name="help_spoons">Keeps small teaspoon and tablespoon measures when converting to metric, rather than replacing them with millilitres. Scaling still adjusts the amount.</string>
    <string name="help_metric_volume">Choose millilitres (ml) or decilitres (dl) for metric volume. Auto chooses ml, dl or litres according to the amount. Saved recipe quantities are preserved.</string>
    <string name="help_rounding">Kitchen uses convenient measures for cooking. Exact keeps more detail. Custom rounds converted quantities using your chosen step; a step of 0.5 gives half-unit increments. This affects display, rather than the saved recipe.</string>
    <string name="help_step_conversion">Converts measurements written in recipe instructions to the selected display units. The original instruction text is preserved.</string>
    <string name="help_ai_import">Choose a content type, copy its prompt into your preferred AI, and describe what you want. Paste the JSON reply here to preview it. For a complete training program, request linked routines and exercise definitions in the same reply.</string>
    <string name="help_content_export">FTM JSON is portable content: notes, recipes, lists, exercises, routines, plans and finished workouts. It includes links to any photos, rather than the photo files. Use a ZIP backup to include photos, settings, a live workout and its rest timer.</string>
    <string name="help_backup_retention">After a successful folder backup, keep this many recent FTM backup files in that folder. The number counts backups, rather than days. Manual ZIP exports saved elsewhere are unaffected.</string>
    <string name="help_trash_retention">Trashed items remain restorable until this many days have passed. After that they are permanently erased. Never keeps them until you empty the trash yourself.</string>
    <string name="help_checked_section">Moves completed checklist items into a separate section. Turn it off to keep checked items in their original positions.</string>
    <string name="help_progression_increment">The amount of weight to add when a load increase is suggested. Choose its unit below. Suggested loads are rounded to the exercise’s own increment.</string>
    <string name="help_prs">Personal records compare completed strength sets with earlier finished workouts. They track greatest weight, estimated one-rep maximum (e1RM), and single-set volume: weight multiplied by reps. Warmups are excluded.</string>
